<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Timbuktu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Timbuktu is a city near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger River</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, at the southern edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>. It became a major centre of the trans-Saharan trade and of Islamic learning under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> and reached its height under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a>, when its mosques and scholars were famous throughout the Islamic world. Its prosperity declined after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moroccan-invasion-of-songhai">Moroccan invasion of 1591</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/timbuktu.jpg" alt="Timbuktu" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Flickr Emilio Labrador Santiago de Chile 3059349393 (Flickr) · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Donkeys,_Timbuktu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son Monzon (r. c. 1790–1808) fought repeated wars with Kaarta: in 1796 Park found the roads between the two kingdoms closed because Monzon’s army had marched against Kaarta’s king, Desse Koro, whose fortress of Gedingooma it did not take, and around 1800 Monzon’s power reached <b>Timbuktu</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bamana-empire">Bamana Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…the 1880s and 1890s French columns pushed east to the Niger, defeating the Tukulor state of Ahmadu, taking <b>Timbuktu</b> in 1894 and fighting a long war against Samori Ture, who was captured in 1898, while other expeditions conquered the Kingdom of Dahomey in 1892–1894 and occupied the Mossi…</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-west-africa">French West Africa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Cities such as <b>Timbuktu</b>, Djenné and Gao became centres of trade, Islamic scholarship and the arts.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mali-empire">Mali Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…Ahmad al-Mansur sent an expedition of a few thousand men, many of them musketeers, under the Spanish-born eunuch Judar Pasha across the Sahara; it defeated the much larger Songhai army at the Battle of Tondibi in March 1591 and occupied Gao and <b>Timbuktu</b>, creating the Pashalik of Timbuktu.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saadi-sultanate">Saadi Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The traveller Leo Africanus, visiting <b>Timbuktu</b> around 1510, reported that books were sold there at greater profit than any other merchandise.</blockquote><a href="/empire/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Arabic spread further with trade and religion: across the Sahara and the Sahel, where scholars of cities such as <b>Timbuktu</b> wrote in it, along the coast of East Africa and to South and Southeast Asia.</blockquote><a href="/language/arabic">Arabic · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li></ul></section>
